{{#> layout pageTitle="Contents" description="A reference guide to signing transactions with bitcoin core and using BIP 39 backups."}}
<p class="eyebrow">THE GUIDE</p>
<h1>Bitcoin Core BIP39 Signer</h1>
<p class="lead">How-to guide for restoring a BIP39 wallet securely and privately.</p>
<p>The guide consists of getting the fundamentals right. A core pillar of this guide is understanding that in bitcoin self-custody sometimes less is more. One of the main things about bitcoin is to obfuscate to the public that you own any of it. If someone enters your house whether it is a friend, family, law enforcement, criminals, they won't find anything that points to you owning bitcoin in plain sight. Everything related to bitcoin will be obfuscated as explained in this guide. Among a few things it means not purchasing bitcoin specific products. This includes hardware wallets, hardware backups like metal stamping kits, bitcoin subscription services, random merchandise like clothes, posters, toys, etc. But it also means not running the cutting edge software for your bitcoin node, just good old Bitcoin Core does the job. No need for Sparrow, Electrs, or any other software. The goal is to minimize attack surface.</p>

